insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('33011e71-74ef-59c1-b7fc-fe66df57b856', 'wg12-paragraph-transitions-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-paragraph-transitions'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Box That Changed Trade$q$, $q$(1) For most of the history of ocean shipping, loading a cargo ship was slow and costly work. (2) Dockworkers carried sacks, barrels, and crates aboard one at a time, fitting each piece into the hold wherever it would go. (3) A single ship could sit in port for a week or more while its cargo was packed and later unpacked by hand. (4) ____, goods were often damaged or lost during the many times they were handled between the factory and the store.

(5) The solution that emerged in the middle of the twentieth century was surprisingly simple: a steel box of standard size that could be filled at the factory and sealed shut. (6) Cranes could lift the box from a truck or train directly onto a ship, and at the destination the process ran in reverse. (7) Because every box had the same dimensions and the same corner fittings, any crane in any port equipped for containers could handle it. (8) ____, ships could be designed with cells that held stacked containers securely, allowing a single vessel to carry thousands of them.

(9) The effects reached far beyond the docks. (10) Loading time dropped from days to hours, and the cost of moving goods across oceans fell sharply. (11) Manufacturers could now build factories far from their customers and still ship products cheaply. (12) Some of the earliest containers were painted in bright colors so that they could be identified from a distance. (13) Over time, this shift helped create the global supply chains that stock store shelves today, linking producers and buyers on opposite sides of the world.$q$, 'draft')
