   $q$Which of the following is a complete sentence rather than a fragment?$q$, $q$[{"text":"To restore the faded film, the technicians scanned every frame by hand.","is_correct":true},{"text":"To restore the faded film by scanning every frame by hand.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"infinitive-fragment"},{"text":"Every frame of the faded film that the technicians scanned by hand.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"noun-phrase-fragment"},{"text":"The technicians, having scanned every frame of the faded film by hand.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"participle-fragment"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$To restore the faded film, the technicians scanned every frame by hand.$q$, $q$The introductory infinitive phrase is followed by a main clause with the subject "technicians" and the verb "scanned." "Having scanned" is a participle, not a main verb, so the choice beginning "The technicians, having scanned" is a fragment.$q$, 'draft', null)
