   $q$Which sentence, placed in the blank at sentence 11, best explains how the final line supports the writer's claim?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The poem ends with a quotation, which is a common way for poets to finish a work.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"summary-not-analysis"},{"text":"The last line shows that the speaker plans to reopen the store under a new name.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The last line shows that the speaker was tired and simply lost track of her count.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"By refusing to make \"one store\" smaller, the speaker shows the place is more than the total of its parts.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$By refusing to make "one store" smaller, the speaker shows the place is more than the total of its parts.$q$, $q$The claim in sentence 2 is that the store cannot be reduced to counted objects, and the speaker's refusal to shrink "one store" shows exactly that. Reading the line as simple tiredness ignores the poem's movement from goods to memories.$q$, 'draft', '75148bc6-f525-5088-86a9-1ccd2e3c3fb6')
